  "post": {
    "caption": "Tourmaline was once called an ash puller. In the early 1700s, Dutch traders brought it from Ceylon, now Sri Lanka, and reportedly used heated stones to pull ash from their pipes. It can become electrically charged when heated or squeezed, and it has been used in pressure gauges. Its name is said to mean mixed gems in Sinhalese, and it has one of the widest color ranges of any gem. Tourmaline is an October birthstone.",
